WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.458
Imagine standing in a quiet
Siberian forest just after sunrise.

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:05.805
The air is still.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.348
The trees stretch to the horizon.

00:00:08.557 --> 00:00:12.272
Then something brighter
than the Sun tears across the sky.

00:00:12.713 --> 00:00:17.821
Seconds later, a tremendous blast sends
a shock wave racing through the forest.

00:00:18.065 --> 00:00:20.828
Trees snap. The ground trembles.

00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:25.089
And in a moment, an enormous
area of taiga is devastated.

00:00:25.414 --> 00:00:27.307
But here is the strange part.

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:30.395
When scientists eventually
reached the site,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.471
they did not find the giant
crater they expected.

00:00:33.854 --> 00:00:36.664
How could an explosion
devastate so much forest,

00:00:36.815 --> 00:00:39.334
without a giant rock striking the ground?

00:00:40.031 --> 00:00:45.116
This is the story of Tunguska, and the missing
crater is the clue that unlocks it.

00:00:45.557 --> 00:00:48.437
To see the mystery,
start with a modern image.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.010
In July 2024, NASA's Landsat 8
satellite photographed the region

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.623
in central Siberia.

00:00:55.948 --> 00:01:01.881
From orbit, it looks almost peaceful:
green forest, rivers, and patches of wetland.

00:01:02.264 --> 00:01:05.190
The scar from the disaster
is no longer obvious.

00:01:05.573 --> 00:01:09.126
Now imagine rewinding
this landscape more than a century,

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.725
to the morning of June
thirtieth, nineteen oh eight.

00:01:13.422 --> 00:01:17.497
This is a remote part of what
is now Russia's Krasnoyarsk region,

00:01:17.775 --> 00:01:20.480
near the Podkamennaya Tunguska River.

00:01:21.177 --> 00:01:25.380
The Evenki people lived and travelled
across this enormous landscape.

00:01:25.891 --> 00:01:29.374
A few witnesses later described
a powerful light in the sky,

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.507
followed by thunderous noises,
intense heat, and a violent blast.

00:01:34.018 --> 00:01:36.003
Some reports were written down much later,

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.975
so scientists treat the details carefully.

00:01:39.184 --> 00:01:42.818
But there is no serious doubt
that a catastrophic event occurred.

00:01:43.143 --> 00:01:46.104
The atmosphere itself
became part of the disaster.

00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:51.189
As the shock wave travelled outward,
it damaged a vast area of forest.

00:01:51.793 --> 00:01:56.634
Later surveys mapped a broad, irregular
pattern of trees knocked to the ground.

00:01:56.959 --> 00:02:01.522
The most commonly cited affected area
is about two thousand square kilometres.

00:02:01.963 --> 00:02:05.957
That is not the footprint of one
fallen tree, or a local fire.

00:02:06.398 --> 00:02:08.581
It is landscape-scale destruction.

00:02:08.906 --> 00:02:11.623
And no camera recorded
the event as it happened.

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:15.814
Every moving image of the explosion
you see here is a reconstruction.

00:02:16.197 --> 00:02:20.052
For years, the exact location
remained difficult to investigate.

00:02:20.377 --> 00:02:23.454
The distance was formidable,
access was limited,

00:02:23.698 --> 00:02:28.783
and the early twentieth century brought
war, revolution, and civil conflict.

00:02:29.224 --> 00:02:33.055
The Russian mineralogist
Leonid Kulik tried to reach the site

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:37.049
in nineteen twenty-one, but was unable
to get all the way there.

00:02:37.432 --> 00:02:40.973
He finally reached the devastated
region in nineteen twenty-seven,

00:02:41.147 --> 00:02:43.747
almost nineteen years after the explosion.

00:02:43.747 --> 00:02:45.976
Imagine what the expedition encountered.

00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:48.554
Long rows of fallen trunks.

00:02:48.797 --> 00:02:51.375
Broken, burned, and stripped trees.

00:02:51.758 --> 00:02:54.881
Even in photographs taken
in nineteen twenty-nine,

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.783
the extraordinary damage is unmistakable.

00:02:58.109 --> 00:02:59.676
In some parts of the forest,

00:02:59.827 --> 00:03:03.693
the fallen trees pointed generally
away from the central area.

00:03:04.076 --> 00:03:06.491
Near the center, other
trunks were still standing,

00:03:06.526 --> 00:03:09.870
but with their branches torn
away, like telegraph poles.

00:03:10.311 --> 00:03:13.608
The distribution was more complicated
than a perfect circle.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.683
But it told investigators
that a powerful pressure wave had swept

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.007
across the terrain.

00:03:19.611 --> 00:03:21.666
Kulik was searching for a meteorite.

00:03:22.107 --> 00:03:28.016
And a meteorite striking Earth with enormous
force usually leaves clues: a crater,

00:03:28.191 --> 00:03:31.638
scattered material,
and a disturbed geological surface.

00:03:32.021 --> 00:03:36.236
Yet there was no great impact crater
to match the scale of the destruction.

00:03:36.514 --> 00:03:38.929
The contrast was difficult to explain.

00:03:39.208 --> 00:03:42.401
The evidence pointed
to a cosmic catastrophe.

00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:47.532
The ground did not show the kind
of collision scar the expedition had expected.

00:03:48.043 --> 00:03:49.680
Here is the essential difference.

00:03:50.005 --> 00:03:54.254
An asteroid does not have to hit
the ground to do immense damage.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:58.991
When a space rock enters Earth's
atmosphere at tremendous speed,

00:03:59.374 --> 00:04:01.615
the air in front of it compresses.

00:04:02.312 --> 00:04:04.993
Stress builds inside the object.

00:04:05.504 --> 00:04:09.684
It may fragment and release a large
fraction of its energy in the sky.

00:04:10.288 --> 00:04:12.691
Scientists call this an airburst.

00:04:13.202 --> 00:04:17.486
Picture the incoming body now,
not as a giant movie asteroid,

00:04:17.765 --> 00:04:20.679
but as an object perhaps
tens of metres across.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.071
It plunges into the atmosphere.

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:24.638
Pressure rises.

00:04:24.766 --> 00:04:26.333
The body breaks apart.

00:04:26.716 --> 00:04:30.222
A brilliant fireball expands
kilometres above the forest.

00:04:30.547 --> 00:04:35.029
A powerful shock wave moves out
from that point and reaches the ground.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:39.116
The pressure pushes against
the trees, breaks branches,

00:04:39.162 --> 00:04:42.134
and can flatten entire stands of forest.

00:04:42.413 --> 00:04:46.929
The surface is devastated even though
the main explosion took place above it.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.576
No enormous crater is required.

00:04:50.017 --> 00:04:51.910
How powerful was this event?

00:04:52.351 --> 00:04:54.696
The exact value is not known.

00:04:55.079 --> 00:04:59.839
Scientific models examined
in a NASA-associated workshop explored energies

00:04:59.909 --> 00:05:02.707
of roughly ten to twenty megatons of TNT

00:05:03.311 --> 00:05:07.548
and scenarios involving a stony
body several dozen metres wide.

00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:10.579
Those figures
are model-dependent estimates,

00:05:10.637 --> 00:05:13.307
not measurements captured
in nineteen oh eight.

00:05:14.004 --> 00:05:19.274
NASA's Earth Observatory also describes
a likely burst altitude of roughly six

00:05:19.332 --> 00:05:20.621
to ten kilometres.

00:05:21.062 --> 00:05:26.612
The precise path, size, composition,
and energy remain uncertain.

00:05:26.612 --> 00:05:29.445
The trees themselves
help explain the physics.

00:05:30.049 --> 00:05:34.902
The burst created a complex field
of pressure, heat, and fast-moving air.

00:05:35.505 --> 00:05:39.708
Areas away from the center
experienced strong outward forces.

00:05:40.219 --> 00:05:41.984
Very close to the central region,

00:05:42.111 --> 00:05:46.372
some trunks remained standing even
after branches and bark were stripped away.

00:05:47.185 --> 00:05:51.504
The resulting treefall pattern,
reconstructed from field surveys,

00:05:51.783 --> 00:05:55.776
helped scientists test how
an airburst could have happened.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.247
This is why an absence can be evidence.

00:05:59.851 --> 00:06:03.903
The missing crater does not prove
that nothing arrived from space.

00:06:04.147 --> 00:06:07.049
It helps reveal
where the energy was released.

00:06:07.653 --> 00:06:11.821
Over the decades, researchers
have explored other possibilities.

00:06:12.146 --> 00:06:14.573
Some have asked whether fragments
might have produced

00:06:14.619 --> 00:06:16.755
a small crater near Lake Cheko.

00:06:17.266 --> 00:06:21.434
Others have studied tiny particles,
the forest's growth history,

00:06:21.643 --> 00:06:23.954
and different simulated flight paths.

00:06:24.337 --> 00:06:26.206
These questions are still debated.

00:06:26.531 --> 00:06:31.535
But they do not overturn the main
scientific picture: the Tunguska destruction

00:06:31.593 --> 00:06:37.340
was most likely caused by an incoming
cosmic object exploding in the atmosphere.

00:06:37.944 --> 00:06:41.159
And we now have a much
more recent example of an airburst.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:43.714
In February twenty thirteen,

00:06:43.923 --> 00:06:47.685
a smaller asteroid exploded
over Chelyabinsk, Russia.

00:06:48.196 --> 00:06:50.703
That event was captured on modern cameras,

00:06:50.819 --> 00:06:54.221
and its shock wave shattered
windows and injured people.

00:06:54.825 --> 00:06:58.981
It was not Tunguska, and the footage
must never be labelled as such.

00:06:59.492 --> 00:07:03.776
But it demonstrates that an object
can release dangerous energy in the air,

00:07:03.869 --> 00:07:06.585
without digging an enormous
hole in the ground.

00:07:07.189 --> 00:07:10.556
Return now to the Landsat image
from twenty twenty-four.

00:07:11.067 --> 00:07:15.339
What was once a vast field of damaged
trees is mostly forest again.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:19.391
The evidence that surprised Kulik
is harder to see from above.

00:07:19.774 --> 00:07:21.887
But the scientific lesson remains.

00:07:22.213 --> 00:07:26.798
Space objects do not always need to reach
the ground to change the landscape.

00:07:27.309 --> 00:07:31.094
This is one reason astronomers
track near-Earth asteroids and work

00:07:31.164 --> 00:07:36.365
on planetary defense: we want to identify
hazardous objects before they arrive.

00:07:36.876 --> 00:07:41.288
On that morning in nineteen oh eight,
the sky flashed, the forest fell,

00:07:41.462 --> 00:07:43.401
and the great crater never appeared.

00:07:43.842 --> 00:07:48.497
A century later, the strongest
explanation is not a hidden spaceship,

00:07:48.613 --> 00:07:51.678
a mystery weapon,
or a hole no one has found.

00:07:51.957 --> 00:07:56.984
It is something more unsettling,
and more real: an explosion in the sky,

00:07:57.135 --> 00:07:59.817
powerful enough to reshape
the Earth below.

00:08:00.142 --> 00:08:03.288
The object did not need
to strike the ground.

00:08:03.613 --> 00:08:06.272
The shock wave did the damage.
